<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    What two or more of the sample's modules mean the same thing by, and none of them owns: today one value object, a range
    of calendar days, which Projects plans a project with and Inspections says an inspection's days with. A type
    one module owns is not shared: it stays in that module's contracts project, as a project's id does.

    Neither DDD_Module nor [assembly: Module], on purpose. The boundary analyzer keeps a module from naming another
    module's types; what is declared here belongs to no module, so every module may name it and nobody publishes
    anything. It references no module and neither Entity Framework nor ASP.NET Core: a module's domain and
    contracts projects reference it, and each module's infrastructure project says how it stores what it keeps
    of it. Keep it small: a type belongs here only when changing it is a change to every module at once.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\..\Source\DDDToolkit\DDDToolkit.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The generator. A NuGet consumer gets it through the package above; inside this repository it is wired by hand. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\..\Source\DDDToolkit.Analyzers\DDDToolkit.Analyzers.csproj" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
